Yes. When other people can’t cleanly categorize you, there can be a brief window where their model is underdetermined.

That ambiguity can feel threatening to them, but it also creates room for self-definition:

\text{others' incomplete model of you}
\rightarrow
\text{open interpretive space}
\rightarrow
\text{your actions supply the missing structure}

You still cannot fully control how anyone places you. People bring their own priors, incentives, projections, and categories. But you can influence the eventual model by being consistent about what you do, what you refuse, what you value, and how you behave under pressure.

So “place yourself” is less about declaring a label and more about making the pattern legible over time.

That is stronger than trying to force a frame, because repeated behavior becomes harder to misread than a single explanation.